  "dataCard": {},
  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/rivulus_rectocaudatus_en/",
      "html": "<p>The killifishes of the genus Rivulus represent the most species-rich genus of New World Killis. R. rectocaudatus from Peru are unique due to their amazing orange anal and caudal fins, as well as their orange scales along the body.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/rivulus_rectocaudatus_en/",
      "html": "<p>Origin: Peru.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/rivulus_rectocaudatus_en/",
      "html": "<p>Rivulus are not annual fish at all, but live as long as any fish of comparable size, may it be a tetra, livebearer or anything else. A very special thing in Rivulus is their tendency to travel over land when the weather is humid enough for that. In the wild, they jump from plant to plant, attaching their body on wet leaves. If they try to do the very same in a living room, this is usually fatal, so it is absolutely necessary to cover the tank. The fish will find even the smallest opening!</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/rivulus_rectocaudatus_en/",
      "html": "<p>The sexes of the imported population are not yet easy to distinguish, as the fish are only 3-4 cm and all of them show the typical Rivulus-spot at the base of the caudal fin, which is typical for adult females. However, the males can already be recognized by the red border of the anal fin, which is completely orange in females. Adult males will get a bluish body, lose the caudal spot and develop a very characteristic spade-shaped caudal fin, which stays round in females.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/rivulus_rectocaudatus_en/",
      "html": "<p>Lexicon: Rivulus: Latin, means \"small brook\". Probably the name was given because the describer was told the fish live in brooks. rectocaudatus: Latin, recto means \"straight\", caudatus means \"tailed\", referring to the characteristic shape of the caudal fin.</p>"
    }
  ],
